## Survival Modelling 


### LifeSphere data extraction
Data was retrieved from LifeSphere  official Neo4j Python driver \needcite{}. Five Cypher queries were issued, one per node family the study requires: Subjects, Survival records, Diagnoses (carrying the primary-diagnosis edge flag and the linked Condition inline), Samples, and PathologyDetails. Each query is parameterised by the pinned Study list and returns flat rows rather than graph objects. Critically, no type casting, filtering on numeric predicates, or derivation of any kind occurs inside Cypher: the queries return every property exactly as the graph stores it, which is uniformly as strings and nulls. The data (Cypher query outputs) are stored in JSON Lines files (one JSON object per row). JSON was chosen over CSV because it distinguishes the string "0" from the number 0 and both from null.

### Data preprocessing

Each raw table is read and typed into data/interim/, with an explicit dtype manifest written alongside each table and enforced on read, so a type that drifts fails loudly instead of silently arriving as an object column. Several schema hazards are resolved here and pinned by tests: (1) all survival properties are stored as strings; (2) the endpoint property is named survivalType; (3) the primary-diagnosis flag is stored as the strings 'True'/'False'; (4) missing race and ethnicity arrive as true nulls in the current graph but as the literal string "None" in an earlier snapshot; and (5) ageAtIndexDays holds years while ageAtDiagnosisDays holds days.
### Cohort definition

Studies are included where pathologic staging is ≥86% present, yielding 20 Studies. Head-and-neck carcinoma sits at 85.98%, so a literal >= 0.86 threshold would yield 19; the Study list is therefore pinned explicitly rather than recomputed from a threshold. A patient is eligible if they carry an overall-survival record with time > 0, which excludes 73 day-zero records following the TCGA Clinical Data Resource convention. The resulting cohort is 6,811 patients with 2,091 events across 20 Studies (69.3% censored).

### Cross-validation and splits

Five-fold cross-validation, split at patient level with a fixed seed and stratified by Study only — jointly stratifying on event is unachievable when testicular cancer holds 4 events across 133 patients. The fold assignment is persisted rather than re-derived, since a seed alone is not sufficient once library versions move. All models train on the same splits. The nested validation slice is the next fold in rotation rather than a second random split, so validation inherits the outer stratification by construction and the train/validation/test sets are disjoint for free. Studies holding zero events in a given fold are flagged and reported pooled rather than dropped.

### Feature construction

Every model draws its features from a single fitted specification, so no model encodes a field its own way. The baseline and flattened model read the cohort roster plus the typed Subject, reduced-Diagnosis and Sample tables, assembling one row per patient; the graph model reads the roster plus the typed Subject, unreduced Diagnosis, Sample and PathologyDetail tables and reconstructs one subgraph per patient from them. Node features in the graph model are sliced out of the same fitted encoders the other models use rather than re-encoded, so the only difference between models is which rows the encoders see.

Where a patient carries several diagnoses, the flattened representation reduces to the primary diagnosis with a per-field fallback to the patient's other diagnoses. The fallback is not cosmetic: primary-strict leaves melanoma 79.1% stage-missing (337/426 patients) against 10.3% under the fallback, and a stage column that is 79% missing in exactly one Study reintroduces the cancer-type confound the cohort restriction exists to prevent. Under the primary rule every diagnosis carries a Condition, so there is no missing-Condition category to model.

Every fitted statistic, category vocabularies, imputation values, standardisation constants, is computed on that fold's training patients alone, frozen, and applied unchanged to training and held-out patients alike. This is applied to vocabularies and rare-level thresholds as well as to continuous features, since a category vocabulary learned from held-out patients is the same class of leak as a mean.

Stage is collapsed to an ordinal score over 0/I/II/III/IV and enters as a single continuous covariate, a 1-degree-of-freedom model. Stage X, meaning "cannot be assessed", becomes missing rather than acquiring a rank; Stage IS strips to I under the same suffix rule as every other sub-stage. Missing stage (276 patients, 4.1%, spread across 17 of 20 Studies at a maximum of 14.0%) is imputed to the training-fold within-Study median with no missingness indicator.

Age is taken as ageAtDiagnosisDays / 365.25, falling back to ageAtIndexDays for the 63 patients lacking it and to the training-fold within-Study median for the remaining 20. conditionSubtype is one-hot encoded with training-fold levels of n < 20 lumped to a rare category, leaving ~30 named levels covering 96% of patients. Race is one-hot encoded with two levels of n = 9 and n = 1 folded into "other". Sample composition enters as a normalised proportion vector over the three baseline biospecimen types. The Condition node is keyed on its code rather than its name field, in which 173 codes collapse to 54 names.

Survival records are excluded on the basis of the immortal-time audit. Guard functions assert that no survival-derived column, no Study identifier and no observation-window column reaches any design matrix, matching case-insensitively and covering one-hot expansions and alternative endpoints; they execute inside the feature pipeline itself as well as in the test suite, so no model can produce a design matrix without them running.

Survival objective and decoder

Every model is trained against the Cox partial likelihood \cite{cox1972} with Efron tie handling \cite{efron1977}, stratified by Study over 20 strata and optimised full-batch. Training proceeds in two stages: the encoder is trained, its weights frozen, and a shared stratified Cox decoder refit on the resulting embedding. Stage one differs between models because that is the representation under test; stage two is a single shared implementation, since a difference there would enter the comparison as though it were a structural finding. Two ablated controls test the dual-stage process itself: a randomly initialised encoder passed to the same decoder, and a single-stage variant. Because the training loss and the decoder come from different libraries, the two were verified to implement the identical objective to 4.1 × 10⁻⁵; Breslow tie handling differs by 1.27 and is therefore not used. Within-Study Harrell's C \cite{harrell1982} is the primary metric, reported alongside pooled Harrell's C, Uno's IPCW C \cite{uno2011}, time-dependent AUC at 1, 2 and 3 years, and the integrated Brier score \cite{graf1999} over [0, 3 yr].

Baseline Cox model

The clinical baseline is a penalised Study-stratified Cox model over sex, race, stage ordinal, age at diagnosis and histological subtype, taking a column subset of the shared feature specification. The penalty is selected per fold on the nested validation slice by recomputing the unpenalised partial likelihood rather than by reading the fitted log-likelihood, which nets off the penalty. The C-index is nearly flat across five orders of magnitude of the penalty (0.6901 against 0.6886 at the extremes), so the fairness axis of the comparison lies in the covariate set and model class rather than in regularisation.

Flattened neural control

The flattened control holds one row per patient over the identical feature specification, giving 167 columns once the Condition feature is keyed on code. These pass through a single 32-unit hidden layer to a 16-dimensional embedding, with dropout 0.2 and Adam at 10⁻³ with weight decay 10⁻⁴, trained for up to 500 epochs with early stopping at patience 30.

Graph model

The construction gives one rooted heterogeneous subgraph per patient over 5 node types (Subject, Diagnosis, Condition, Sample, PathologyDetail) and 5 forward relations, plus 5 explicitly added reverse relations. Reverse edges are mandatory rather than optional: every relation in the LifeSphere schema points away from the Subject, so without them the root receives no messages, and a test asserts that dropping them leaves the model at exactly chance. Survival nodes are excluded as the prediction label and Study nodes as both the split key and the cancer-type confound; Intervention and PhenotypeObservation are excluded as node types rather than merely as features.

The encoder applies a type-aware linear input embedding to d = 32, followed by L = 2 relation-aware message-passing layers \cite{schlichtkrull2018rgcn} with mean aggregation, an identity residual, per-type layer normalisation and a root-only readout. L = 2 is fixed by the construction rather than tuned, being the eccentricity of the root: Diagnosis and Sample lie at one hop, Condition and PathologyDetail at two, so two layers is exactly what the root needs to see the whole record and a third would add only over-smoothing. The graph's diameter is 3 (Condition → Diagnosis → Subject → Sample), so the root is the only node whose receptive field covers the record at this depth, and the readout is therefore a selection rather than a pooling operation. Subgraphs are rebuilt per fold rather than cached once, because node features carry that fold's fitted encoders; the cache is keyed on a fingerprint over the patient set, the build options, the fitted encoders and the builder source, so any change to these invalidates it rather than silently reusing a stale build. The resulting embedding is frozen and passed to the shared decoder.
